WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:06.170
KURTIS VAN GENT: Hi, everyone.

00:00:06.170 --> 00:00:09.040
Welcome to Connecting to
Cloud SQL from Kubernetes.

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:10.540
My name is Kurtis Van Gent.

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:12.230
I'm a Developer
Programs Engineer.

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:14.560
I work on Google Cloud
Platform to make the developer

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:17.860
experience better for
developers like you.

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:20.090
Today, we're going to be
talking about how to connect

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:21.870
to Cloud SQL from Kubernetes.

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:23.805
We're going to have
three main points.

00:00:23.805 --> 00:00:25.180
The first is we're
going to cover

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:28.115
how to connect to
Cloud SQL in general,

00:00:28.115 --> 00:00:30.490
then we're going to narrow
down and talk about connecting

00:00:30.490 --> 00:00:33.460
from Kubernetes to Cloud SQL.

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:35.710
And then finally, we're going
to narrow down even more

00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:38.920
and do a hands-on example
of the best practices

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:42.070
for connecting from Kubernetes.

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:43.900
Before we begin,
there's some basics

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:45.940
that you should understand
before we start.

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:49.450
First, it's expected that you
know and understand Cloud SQL

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:50.825
and how a managed
database works,

00:00:50.825 --> 00:00:53.408
what some of the benefits are,
and when you should try and use

00:00:53.408 --> 00:00:54.100
one.

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:57.280
Second, it's understood
that you should understand

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:01.240
the basics of a container,
how container images work,

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:03.820
and what some of the benefits
for containerization are.

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:06.190
And finally, we hope
that you understand

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:08.230
some of the basics, at
least, of Kubernetes

00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:13.730
and you understand some concepts
such as pod and deployments.

00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:16.858
First, let's talk about
connecting to Cloud SQL.

00:01:16.858 --> 00:01:19.025
When you're connecting,
there's two different things

00:01:19.025 --> 00:01:20.120
you need to think about.

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:23.120
First, the path-- how am I
going to reach the instance?

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:24.920
And the second is
authorization--

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:28.170
how do I get permission
to talk to the instance?

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:30.990
When you're selecting a path,
you have two different options.

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:32.930
The first option is public IP.

00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:35.690
Public IP is a
static IPv4 address

00:01:35.690 --> 00:01:37.618
that's accessible from
the public internet.

00:01:37.618 --> 00:01:39.410
As long as you have
access to the internet,

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:42.170
you can potentially connect
to your Cloud SQL instance.

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:44.798
By default, authorization
is required,

00:01:44.798 --> 00:01:46.340
since we don't want
anyone connecting

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:49.860
to your application that
has access to the internet.

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:51.560
Some of the benefits
of public IP

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:54.408
is that you can connect
from outside GCP,

00:01:54.408 --> 00:01:56.450
while one of the drawbacks
is you may have higher

00:01:56.450 --> 00:01:58.867
latency, since you're connecting
over the internet instead

00:01:58.867 --> 00:02:01.710
of using an internal network.

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:05.270
The second option you have is
to connect using private IP.

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:07.580
Private IP is an
IPv4 address that's

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:12.020
peered into your project via a
Virtual Private Cloud or VPC,

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:13.220
for short.

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:15.930
By default, authorization
is not required,

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:18.950
but you do have to be on the
VPC to access your Cloud SQL

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:20.700
instance.

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:22.220
One of the benefits
of private IP

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:23.858
is you usually
have lower latency.

00:02:23.858 --> 00:02:26.150
This is because you're not
connecting over the internet

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:30.872
and instead connecting directly
through an internal network.

00:02:30.872 --> 00:02:32.330
Once you've decided
how to connect,

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:34.395
the next step is authorization.

00:02:34.395 --> 00:02:36.020
When it comes to
authorization, there's

00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:38.400
three different options
you have to look at.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:40.520
The first is the
Cloud SQL Proxy.

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:42.530
The Cloud SQL Proxy
is a binary that

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:44.530
provides IAM authentication.

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:46.460
It uses a service
account to provide

00:02:46.460 --> 00:02:49.070
authorization and
strong encryption using

00:02:49.070 --> 00:02:51.740
short-lived SSL certificates.

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:54.470
The second option you can
use is authorized networks.

00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:57.040
This is where you specify
a list of IP addresses

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:58.800
that are authorized to connect.

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:00.650
For private IP, there
is a similar feature

00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:05.090
called VPC egress firewall
rules that you can use instead.

00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:07.400
The third option is
self-managed SSL.

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:09.890
You can create SSL certificates
for your instance that

00:03:09.890 --> 00:03:11.870
can be distributed to
your application that

00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:13.400
encrypt your connections.

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:15.550
For MySQL and Postgres,
this is a client

00:03:15.550 --> 00:03:19.340
and server-side credential,
while for SQL Server,

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:22.362
this is only a
server-side credential.

00:03:22.362 --> 00:03:24.070
It's important to
remember that these are

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:26.180
options for authorization only.

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:29.000
You still have to authenticate
with the database using

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:31.890
your username the password.

00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:34.260
So to summarize,
you have private IP

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:35.610
and you have public IP.

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:38.670
For public IP, you have to
use either the Cloud SQL

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:41.270
Proxy or authorized networks.

00:03:41.270 --> 00:03:43.770
We also recommend that if you
are using authorized networks,

00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:45.840
that you use self-managed SSL.

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:47.520
This encrypts your
data and makes

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:49.860
sure that no one else can
read it in transit unless they

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:51.930
have the certificates.

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:54.918
However, this is automatically
done by the Cloud SQL Proxy,

00:03:54.918 --> 00:03:56.460
so if you decide to
use it, you won't

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:59.100
have to worry about
managing your own SSL.

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:01.050
When it comes to
public versus private,

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:02.865
use whichever one works best.

00:04:02.865 --> 00:04:05.850
And for authorization, while we
recommend the Cloud SQL Proxy,

00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:07.920
it's perfectly OK to
use authorized networks

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:11.410
and self-managed SSL as well.

00:04:11.410 --> 00:04:14.816
Next, let's talk about
Kubernetes to Cloud SQL.

00:04:14.816 --> 00:04:17.399
There are three different ways
that you're going to see people

00:04:17.399 --> 00:04:18.720
recommending you to connect.

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:21.930
The first is you can connect
directly using private IP.

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:24.630
The second is you can run
the proxy as a service.

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:27.460
And finally, you can run
the proxy as a sidecar.

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:29.810
This is our recommended
solution over the first two.

00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:35.310
Because private IP doesn't
require any authorization,

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:37.020
it's possible to just
connect directly.

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:40.800
You can just put in the private
IP into your application

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:43.950
and connect with that directly
to your Cloud SQL instance.

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:45.840
In order to access
that private IP,

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:49.350
you have to be on a VPC-native
Google Kubernetes Engine

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:50.980
cluster.

00:04:50.980 --> 00:04:53.280
Now, this means that it
has to be a GKE cluster.

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:56.460
If you're using a
cluster off GCP,

00:04:56.460 --> 00:04:58.640
you won't be able to connect.

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:01.410
Now, you may be thinking, if
it's so easy to use private IP,

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:04.750
do I still want
to use the proxy?

00:05:04.750 --> 00:05:07.540
With the proxy, there's the
downside of the initial setup

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:09.790
being a little bit of a
hassle, but the upside

00:05:09.790 --> 00:05:12.550
is you get strong IAM
authentication and strong

00:05:12.550 --> 00:05:16.390
encryption for all the
data from your application.

00:05:16.390 --> 00:05:18.040
For the proxy, the
advantage is you

00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:19.540
don't have to worry
about the setup,

00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:22.510
you can connect
directly via IP, but it

00:05:22.510 --> 00:05:24.350
requires a VPC-native cluster.

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:26.170
So if you're connecting
from off-GKE,

00:05:26.170 --> 00:05:27.620
you don't have that option.

00:05:27.620 --> 00:05:30.160
And there's work authorization--
anyone on your cluster

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:34.120
could potentially
connect to your database.

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:35.950
Why is IAM so important?

00:05:35.950 --> 00:05:38.050
Like, why do we push
the proxy so hard?

00:05:38.050 --> 00:05:39.550
Well, the reason
is is that you can

00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:41.110
use a service
account to represent

00:05:41.110 --> 00:05:42.610
your entire application.

00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:44.860
It gives you a one-to-one
mapping of permissions

00:05:44.860 --> 00:05:47.260
to applications, which
makes it easy to manage.

00:05:47.260 --> 00:05:49.340
If you decide that an
application no longer has

00:05:49.340 --> 00:05:50.980
a need to access
the database, you

00:05:50.980 --> 00:05:53.740
can simply remove the
permission and it no longer can,

00:05:53.740 --> 00:05:57.230
even if changes to
the app are made.

00:05:57.230 --> 00:05:59.920
Finally, it follows the
principle of least privilege.

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:02.660
The app only has the minimum
permissions that are needed,

00:06:02.660 --> 00:06:06.520
which is a great
security advantage.

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:08.827
When you're running
the proxy as a service,

00:06:08.827 --> 00:06:10.660
typically you do it as
a separate deployment

00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:11.808
from your application.

00:06:11.808 --> 00:06:14.350
So you can see on the left here,
we have our application pods

00:06:14.350 --> 00:06:16.550
being deployed,
and on the right,

00:06:16.550 --> 00:06:18.510
we have our proxy
pods being deployed.

00:06:18.510 --> 00:06:21.442
And your application is going to
connect directly to the proxy,

00:06:21.442 --> 00:06:23.650
and then your proxy is going
to route your connection

00:06:23.650 --> 00:06:26.690
right to Cloud SQL.

00:06:26.690 --> 00:06:28.670
One of the downsides
to this is the traffic

00:06:28.670 --> 00:06:30.830
between your application
and proxy is not

00:06:30.830 --> 00:06:33.830
encrypted by default. The proxy
doesn't offer any encryption

00:06:33.830 --> 00:06:37.340
on the client side, it only
encrypts the traffic coming out

00:06:37.340 --> 00:06:40.730
before it gets
connected to Cloud SQL.

00:06:40.730 --> 00:06:42.980
Another disadvantage
is no authorization.

00:06:42.980 --> 00:06:44.450
Anyone on your
cluster can connect

00:06:44.450 --> 00:06:46.450
to the proxy, which means
anyone in your cluster

00:06:46.450 --> 00:06:47.513
can connect to Cloud SQL.

00:06:47.513 --> 00:06:50.180
This negates the advantage I was
talking about before with Cloud

00:06:50.180 --> 00:06:53.890
SQL IAM authentication.

00:06:53.890 --> 00:06:56.737
Another disadvantage is there
is a single point of failure.

00:06:56.737 --> 00:06:59.070
If all of the applications
are using a single deployment

00:06:59.070 --> 00:07:01.560
of the proxy to run, if
that deployment fails,

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:04.230
all of your applications are
no longer able to connect.

00:07:04.230 --> 00:07:05.730
If something goes
wrong in the proxy

00:07:05.730 --> 00:07:07.380
and it stops
operating correctly,

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:12.320
all of your applications fail
instead of just one instance.

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:15.280
Finally, another disadvantage
is the application and proxy

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:18.130
aren't necessarily running
in the same zone or region.

00:07:18.130 --> 00:07:20.800
This adds additional latency
because now your application

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:22.607
has to jump first to
your proxy and then

00:07:22.607 --> 00:07:24.190
to the Cloud SQL
instance, which could

00:07:24.190 --> 00:07:27.010
be in an entirely different
region or zone, as well.

00:07:27.010 --> 00:07:29.320
By keeping the application
and proxy together,

00:07:29.320 --> 00:07:31.060
you can eliminate
this, meaning you only

00:07:31.060 --> 00:07:35.270
have to worry about the latency
to the Cloud SQL instance.

00:07:35.270 --> 00:07:37.867
This is why we recommend
running the proxy as a sidecar.

00:07:37.867 --> 00:07:39.700
It's called a sidecar
pattern because if you

00:07:39.700 --> 00:07:42.090
think of a motorcycle
with a sidecar,

00:07:42.090 --> 00:07:43.580
it's attached to the side.

00:07:43.580 --> 00:07:46.183
You still have your main
application here in the middle

00:07:46.183 --> 00:07:48.100
and then you have the
Cloud SQL Proxy attached

00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:49.510
to the side right here.

00:07:49.510 --> 00:07:51.700
The application can connect
to the Cloud SQL Proxy

00:07:51.700 --> 00:07:55.210
directly, which then
connects to Cloud SQL.

00:07:55.210 --> 00:07:57.602
Because this is a pod,
it's an atomic unit,

00:07:57.602 --> 00:07:59.560
meaning the application
and the Cloud SQL Proxy

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:00.850
are always together.

00:08:00.850 --> 00:08:02.860
If the Cloud SQL Proxy
fails, the application

00:08:02.860 --> 00:08:04.000
has also failed.

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:06.340
If the application fails,
the Cloud SQL Proxy

00:08:06.340 --> 00:08:09.800
is still running
in the other pods.

00:08:09.800 --> 00:08:12.610
It's authorized because the
Cloud SQL Proxy is inside

00:08:12.610 --> 00:08:14.290
the same pod as the application.

00:08:14.290 --> 00:08:15.935
Only the application
can access it.

00:08:15.935 --> 00:08:18.310
And you don't have to worry
about other traffic accessing

00:08:18.310 --> 00:08:21.570
a proxy that it's
not supposed to.

00:08:21.570 --> 00:08:23.350
It's also isolated.

00:08:23.350 --> 00:08:25.237
If the application fails
or the proxy fails,

00:08:25.237 --> 00:08:26.820
you don't have to
worry about the rest

00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:28.530
of your application going down.

00:08:28.530 --> 00:08:32.250
It's just that pod that fails.

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:33.570
And finally, it's encrypted.

00:08:33.570 --> 00:08:35.640
The minute the data leaves
your Cloud SQL Proxy,

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:38.490
it is encrypted and sent
to your Cloud SQL instance

00:08:38.490 --> 00:08:39.679
before it's unencrypted.

00:08:39.679 --> 00:08:42.179
This means you don't have to
worry about anybody potentially

00:08:42.179 --> 00:08:46.250
listening in between your
application and your proxy.

00:08:46.250 --> 00:08:47.930
OK, so you're convinced.

00:08:47.930 --> 00:08:51.860
You want to use the Cloud SQL
Proxy in a sidecar, but how?

00:08:51.860 --> 00:08:53.980
Let's talk about deploying
the proxy as a sidecar.

00:08:56.250 --> 00:08:58.750
Again, there's two different
things you need to think about.

00:08:58.750 --> 00:09:01.042
The first is you need to
decide how to pass credentials

00:09:01.042 --> 00:09:02.033
into the proxy.

00:09:02.033 --> 00:09:03.450
Once you've decided
that, you just

00:09:03.450 --> 00:09:05.310
have to mount the
proxy as a sidecar

00:09:05.310 --> 00:09:08.738
onto an existing application.

00:09:08.738 --> 00:09:10.530
When you're passing
credentials, again, you

00:09:10.530 --> 00:09:12.070
have a couple of
choices to make.

00:09:12.070 --> 00:09:14.170
The first is workload identity.

00:09:14.170 --> 00:09:16.990
This is where you can map a
Google Service Account, or GSA

00:09:16.990 --> 00:09:19.410
for short, to a Kubernetes
Service Account,

00:09:19.410 --> 00:09:20.963
or KSA for short.

00:09:20.963 --> 00:09:22.380
The advantage of
this is you don't

00:09:22.380 --> 00:09:24.460
have to worry about
managing any secrets.

00:09:24.460 --> 00:09:26.327
But it is a GKE-only feature.

00:09:26.327 --> 00:09:28.410
This means you'll have to
use a GKE cluster if you

00:09:28.410 --> 00:09:30.210
want to take advantage of this.

00:09:30.210 --> 00:09:32.040
If you're not
using a GKE cluster

00:09:32.040 --> 00:09:34.140
or if you're operating
somewhere that

00:09:34.140 --> 00:09:36.150
doesn't have workload
identity, you can still

00:09:36.150 --> 00:09:38.268
use a service account key file.

00:09:38.268 --> 00:09:40.560
This is where you have service
account credentials that

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:42.930
are stored in a JSON key file.

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:44.460
The downside is
you have to worry

00:09:44.460 --> 00:09:47.310
about keeping track of that
JSON key file and rotating it,

00:09:47.310 --> 00:09:52.110
but you are able to do this on
a standard Kubernetes instance.

00:09:52.110 --> 00:09:54.692
So option 1, workload
identity for GKE.

00:09:54.692 --> 00:09:56.150
If you look to the
left over there,

00:09:56.150 --> 00:09:58.600
you'll see the application
of Cloud SQL Proxy.

00:09:58.600 --> 00:10:01.330
What happens when you decide
to use application default

00:10:01.330 --> 00:10:03.835
credentials is the client
library, including the Cloud

00:10:03.835 --> 00:10:06.700
SQL Proxy, can reach out
to the metadata server.

00:10:06.700 --> 00:10:09.260
This is true for both the
application and the proxy,

00:10:09.260 --> 00:10:10.690
so you can use one
service account

00:10:10.690 --> 00:10:13.660
to represent both things.

00:10:13.660 --> 00:10:15.517
Once the Cloud SQL
Proxy makes that call,

00:10:15.517 --> 00:10:18.100
it gets the token that it needs
to authorize with the instance

00:10:18.100 --> 00:10:21.383
and uses it to make any
API calls necessary.

00:10:21.383 --> 00:10:23.800
In order to set this up, the
first thing you'll need to do

00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:25.720
is create a Kubernetes
Service Account.

00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:27.520
This is a fairly
simple deploy and can

00:10:27.520 --> 00:10:30.760
be a config.yaml that's stored
along with your application's

00:10:30.760 --> 00:10:33.020
deployment.

00:10:33.020 --> 00:10:35.180
Step two is you need to
bind your Google Service

00:10:35.180 --> 00:10:37.800
Account to the Kubernetes
Service Account.

00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:38.660
This is two steps.

00:10:38.660 --> 00:10:41.060
It's one gcloud
command that specifies

00:10:41.060 --> 00:10:43.100
which Google Service
Account you're using

00:10:43.100 --> 00:10:44.980
and which Kubernetes
Service Account.

00:10:44.980 --> 00:10:47.630
And then it's a
second step in kubectl

00:10:47.630 --> 00:10:51.073
to connect to your
Kubernetes Service Account.

00:10:51.073 --> 00:10:52.990
Finally, once you've
done the first two steps,

00:10:52.990 --> 00:10:55.240
you need to add the
Kubernetes Service

00:10:55.240 --> 00:10:56.577
Account to your application.

00:10:56.577 --> 00:10:58.660
All you need to do is add
the service account name

00:10:58.660 --> 00:11:00.490
that you specified
in the first step

00:11:00.490 --> 00:11:02.070
and then bound in
the second step

00:11:02.070 --> 00:11:03.820
to make sure that your
application uses it

00:11:03.820 --> 00:11:05.186
when it's deployed next.

00:11:05.186 --> 00:11:08.350
GKE will automatically handle
the metadata server for you,

00:11:08.350 --> 00:11:11.290
as long as the option is
enabled on your cluster.

00:11:13.792 --> 00:11:16.250
The second option you have is
the service account key file.

00:11:16.250 --> 00:11:18.680
In this case, both the
application and the proxy

00:11:18.680 --> 00:11:21.530
are going to load the
credentials from a file

00:11:21.530 --> 00:11:24.260
and then use that credential
to generate the tokens needed

00:11:24.260 --> 00:11:27.350
to make any API calls.

00:11:27.350 --> 00:11:30.002
Step one is you have to
create a service account key.

00:11:30.002 --> 00:11:32.210
So once you have your Google
Service Account created,

00:11:32.210 --> 00:11:33.740
you can use a quick
gcloud command

00:11:33.740 --> 00:11:37.150
to create a local key.json file.

00:11:37.150 --> 00:11:40.720
Step two is you want to
turn the key into a secret.

00:11:40.720 --> 00:11:43.450
Using kubectl, you can
create a generic secret

00:11:43.450 --> 00:11:45.940
with whatever name
you prefer, and you

00:11:45.940 --> 00:11:50.290
can use a specific file name and
load it from that local file.

00:11:50.290 --> 00:11:52.390
In this case, we're using
service_account.json,

00:11:52.390 --> 00:11:54.340
and we're loading
it from key.json,

00:11:54.340 --> 00:11:57.180
which we created in step one.

00:11:57.180 --> 00:11:59.520
Step 3 is we want to
mount as a volume.

00:11:59.520 --> 00:12:02.530
What this is going to do is put
inside your pod's file system,

00:12:02.530 --> 00:12:04.950
the file that you've
created as a secret

00:12:04.950 --> 00:12:08.520
and make it accessible for
your application to access.

00:12:08.520 --> 00:12:09.900
Once you've mounted
it as a file,

00:12:09.900 --> 00:12:11.600
both the application
and the proxy

00:12:11.600 --> 00:12:14.220
can read in and use it when
it's creating API calls.

00:12:17.700 --> 00:12:19.320
Now, after you've
decided how you're

00:12:19.320 --> 00:12:20.987
going to mount the
credentials, you just

00:12:20.987 --> 00:12:23.880
need to mount the proxy
itself as a sidecar.

00:12:23.880 --> 00:12:26.220
In this case, the proxy
runs in its own container,

00:12:26.220 --> 00:12:30.058
and it shares a pod with
the main application.

00:12:30.058 --> 00:12:32.100
This means it's really
easy to take your existing

00:12:32.100 --> 00:12:34.980
configuration and add the
Cloud SQL Proxy to it.

00:12:34.980 --> 00:12:37.383
You can see above we have a
pretty standard containers

00:12:37.383 --> 00:12:39.550
deployment here, where you
have a list of containers

00:12:39.550 --> 00:12:41.760
that you're using in
your Cloud SQL Proxy.

00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:44.460
On this top half here, we have
things like the environment

00:12:44.460 --> 00:12:46.890
variables you're using to
configure your database-- see,

00:12:46.890 --> 00:12:49.830
this one is DB_USER, which
is created from a secret.

00:12:49.830 --> 00:12:51.660
You also have the name
of the application

00:12:51.660 --> 00:12:54.030
in the other configuration,
such as image or whatever

00:12:54.030 --> 00:12:55.860
you've deciding to use.

00:12:55.860 --> 00:12:58.350
On top of that, you can specify
the name of the Cloud SQL

00:12:58.350 --> 00:13:01.950
Proxy, so you add as a separate
entry in your list here,

00:13:01.950 --> 00:13:05.440
image cloudsql-docker/gce-proxy.

00:13:05.440 --> 00:13:07.330
This is an image that
we maintain for you

00:13:07.330 --> 00:13:09.125
so that you can use it.

00:13:09.125 --> 00:13:11.750
Second, you just need to add the
command you want the Cloud SQL

00:13:11.750 --> 00:13:13.080
Proxy to run.

00:13:13.080 --> 00:13:15.960
So here we just have
Cloud SQL Proxy.

00:13:15.960 --> 00:13:17.480
If you're using
private IP, you want

00:13:17.480 --> 00:13:19.760
to enable the IP
addresses types flag

00:13:19.760 --> 00:13:22.370
instead of equal to private,
and if you're using a service

00:13:22.370 --> 00:13:24.170
account, you want to make sure
that you add the credential

00:13:24.170 --> 00:13:26.810
file flag, as well, to tell
it where exactly that service

00:13:26.810 --> 00:13:28.310
account is located.

00:13:28.310 --> 00:13:30.110
Finally, you'll use
the instances flag

00:13:30.110 --> 00:13:31.760
to pass in the name
of the instance

00:13:31.760 --> 00:13:34.130
and the TCP port you
want it to listen to.

00:13:34.130 --> 00:13:36.230
You can use whatever
TCP port you want,

00:13:36.230 --> 00:13:39.050
or you can just default to the
standard for MySQL, Postgres,

00:13:39.050 --> 00:13:39.840
or SQL Server.

00:13:42.467 --> 00:13:44.050
If you're interested
in learning more,

00:13:44.050 --> 00:13:46.690
here's some additional resources
you can take a look at.

00:13:46.690 --> 00:13:49.380
The first is the Cloud SQL
Proxy, which is open-source,

00:13:49.380 --> 00:13:51.630
written in Go,
available on GitHub.

00:13:51.630 --> 00:13:54.690
The second is the Cloud
SQL JDBC socket factory.

00:13:54.690 --> 00:13:56.880
This can be used by Java
users to connect directly

00:13:56.880 --> 00:13:58.547
to your Cloud SQL
instance without using

00:13:58.547 --> 00:14:00.468
the proxy in a similar manner.

00:14:00.468 --> 00:14:02.010
If you're interested
in finding more,

00:14:02.010 --> 00:14:03.343
please take a look at the repos.

00:14:03.343 --> 00:14:06.105
And if you encounter any issues,
please open an issue on GitHub

00:14:06.105 --> 00:14:09.020
and let us know.

00:14:09.020 --> 00:14:11.970
Thanks for watching,
and have a great day.

